<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>35: Taylor series</title>
  <introduction>
    <p><md>
<mrow>e^u \amp  = \amp  1 + u + \frac{u^2}{2!} + \frac{u^3}{3!} + \ldots +
          \frac{u^n}{n!} + \ldots</mrow>
<mrow>\sin(u) \amp  = \amp  u - \frac{u^3} {3!} + \frac{u^5}{5!} - \frac{u^7}{7!} +
\ldots + (-1)^n\frac{u^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!} + \ldots</mrow>
<mrow>\cos(u) \amp  = \amp  1 - \frac{u^2}{2!} + \frac{u^4}{4!} - \frac{u^6}{6!} +
\ldots + (-1)^n\frac{u^{2n}}{(2n)!} + \ldots</mrow>
<mrow>\frac 1{1-u} \amp  = \amp  1 + u + u^2 + \ldots + u^n + \ldots</mrow>
</md></p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>We can get new power series from old ones.  For instance, the
  Taylor series for <m>f(z)=e^{3z}</m>, can be found using the series for
  <m>e^u</m> above and setting <m>u = 3z</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What do you find for the Taylor series of <m>f(z) = e^{3z}</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the Taylor series for <m>g(z) = e^{3z} - 1</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>You can factor out a ``<m>z</m>'' from <m>g(z)</m> to write <m>g(z)= zh(z)</m>.
  What is <m>h(0)</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Suppose that <m>C</m> is the unit circle oriented in the counterclockwise
  direction.  Evaluate the contour integral
  <md>
  \oint_C \frac{1}{e^{3z} - 1}~dz = \oint_C\frac{1}{zh(z)}~dz = 
  \oint_C \frac{1/h(z)}{z}.</md></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>If <m>f(z_0) = 0</m>, then the Taylor series looks like
  <md>
  \begin{aligned}
    f(z) \amp  = f(z_0) + f'(z_0)(z-z_0) + \frac{f''(z_0)}{2}(z-z_0)^2 +
    \ldots \\
    \amp  = f'(z_0)(z-z_0) + \frac{f''(z_0)}{2}(z-z_0)^2 +
    \ldots \\
    \amp  = (z-z_0)^k h(z) \\
  \end{aligned}
  </md>
  for some <m>k</m>.  We say that <m>f(z)</m> has a zero of order <m>k</m> in this
  case.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Consider the function <m>f(z) = \sin(z^2)</m> and write out its Taylor
  series.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the order of the zero at <m>z=0</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>g(z) = \sin(z^3) - z^3</m>, what is the order of the zero at <m>z=0</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Suppose that <m>f(z) = \cot(z^2) = \frac{\cos(z^2)}{\sin(z^2)}</m>.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why you can write
  <md>
  \cot(z^2) = \frac{h(z)}{z^2}.
  </md></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate the contour integral <m>\oint_C \cot(z^2)~dz</m> where <m>C</m> is
  the unit circle traversed in the counterclockwise direction.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
